Instructions on building SAC from SVN ( Source Code Repository )
------------------------------------------------------------------------

* Introduction
* Checking out the Source
* Creating the Configure Script
* Building a Source Distribution
* Building a Binary Distribution
* Finding Source Code Differences 

* Introcution
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Building a binary of SAC or creating a distribution of SAC directly
from the repository (hereafter referred to as SVN) is possible 
through a series of scripts and utilities.  These utilities are 
provided to ease the maintanence and provide a seamless way to 
add additional operating system support.

* Checking out the Source
------------------------------------------------------------------------
To check out the source, you need SVN, which is available on most 
current operating systems. To check out the source issue the commands

% svn checkout http://seiscode.iris.washington.edu/svn/sac-dev/

This should create a directory named sac-dev with the entire distribution
and auxillary files included.  At this point to progress further, you
need to create the configure script which facilitates Makefile creation.

* Building a Source Distribution
------------------------------------------------------------------------
To create a source distribution is rather simple.  It will include only
the files required to create the distribution from source.  Run

% ./configure 
% make dist 

This will create a compressed tar file named SAC-${VERSION}.tar.gz 
The internal directory for the tar file will be named sac-${VERSION}

* Building a Binary Distribution
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Building a binary distribution is just as easy as for the source.

% ./configure
% make dist-binary

This will create a compress tar file named SAC-${VERSION}-${OSTYPE}.tar.gz
The internal directory for the tar file will be named sac.  To create the
binary distribution, the source code is required to be compiled and may 
take some time.  This will be done automatically if not already done.

* Finding Source Code Differences 
------------------------------------------------------------------------
If you would like to see the difference between your version of the 
source code and what is currently in SVN, try the command from the SAC 
directory.

% svn diff

or 

% svn diff Readme

To find out the differences between your Readme and the version in SVN

* Updating the Configure Script [Not normally Necessary]
------------------------------------------------------------------------
After making chanes to the build system, the configure script needs updating.
To update the configure script, execute the following command in the SAC
directory/

# Only do this if you know what you are doing
% ./bootstrap

The bootstrap script will run a number of utilities including aclocal,
automake and autoconf.  Simply, it takes the configure.in file and the 
Makefile.am files create ./configure and Makefile.in.  Extra, unnecesary
directoires are removed ( autom4te.cache ).

aclocal: Creates aclocal.m4 ( Useful Macros for Configuration )
automake: Makefile.am -> Makefile.in (all directories)
	  Also creates required files files in the config directory
	  - compile
	  - config.guess
	  - config.sub
	  - install-sh
	  - missing
autoconf: configure.in -> configure ( SAC directory )

A utility script to clean up the mess created by bootstrap is also 
provided.  It should return the distribution to as close to the original 
state as possible.  After running this utility script, the bootstrap script
is required to be run to issue any commands like "make clean". 
It can be run simply as:

% ./bootstrap-clean
